{"Booking cancellation does not await reminder cleanup - the cancellation loop calls async reminder managers without awaiting them and no longer includes reminder cleanup in the awaited promise batch, leaving reminders inconsistent": 0.9694256782531738, "Email cron deletes cancelled reminders using the wrong SendGrid endpoint and ignores its own update/delete workflow - the cron performs provider cancellation plus row deletion inside a broad try/catch where a single failure can skip all later reminders and leave persisted cancelled rows uncleared": 0.7763660550117493, "Email reminders with no referenceId are deleted instead of being marked cancelled - hard-deleting the workflowReminder row immediately bypasses the 'mark cancelled first, final delete later' lifecycle": 0.5712119340896606, "SMS cancellation still deletes rows immediately instead of persisting cancelled state - unlike the email path, it does not set cancelled=true and defer provider cleanup to the cron job, creating inconsistent behavior between email and SMS reminders": 0.763737142086029, "Workflow mutations do not await reminder cleanup - async reminder cancellation is invoked from forEach callbacks without awaiting, so failures can be ignored and stale scheduled reminders or rows can remain": 0.9884125590324402, "Reschedule cleanup is fire-and-forget and its failures are swallowed - deleteScheduledEmailReminder and deleteScheduledSMSReminder are called inside forEach without await, so rejected promises become unhandled and can leave old scheduled reminders active": 0.9525689482688904, "Reschedule cancellation of previous booking is fire-and-forget - async cancellation helpers are launched without awaiting them, diverging from the established pattern where other destructive operations are awaited before proceeding": 0.9678720235824585}